00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.970
That just happens sometimes.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:30.040
For real, isn't it super hard to know 
what your customers want?!

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.030
It <i>is</i> hard!

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:35.070
Huh? And you guys call 
yourselves booksellers?

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:40.970
I asked Section Chief Pestmask the same
 thing when I joined the company.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:42.240
And she said...

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.580
Well, that's just the nature of the business.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.020
Cut and dry.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:46.800
Ouch!

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.360
I've got nothing to say to that!

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.710
Like, we still sell out all the time.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:55.080
Like En*o-sensei's one about the native 
tribesman and the college staffer...

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.150
What a monster of a BL manga!

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.300
Seriously, we ordered and 
stocked 'em, then they were gone.

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.700
Time to get up, Section Chief.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:02.810
All right!

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:05.470
Let's start with what we <i>can</i> do!

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:07.280
Time to put in some orders!

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.920
<i>Section Chief Armor's so badass.</i>

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:11.460
Hello, this is Section Chief Armor calling.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:14.030
Honda, can you handle inquiries?

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:15.180
Got it.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.410
<i>A bookstore employee must 
be quick on their toes.</i>

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:24.750
<i>For example, if the task is responding to 
inquiries, one must do so with sincerity.</i>

00:02:24.970 --> 00:02:25.880
Let's see...

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:31.850
